e uses; and the diff sentence names four files. The "what this round did not do" sentence in round 7's section — which read as though the delivery carried no acknowledgement edit at all — now says that the inherited uncommitted `+11 −3` is what keeps the acknowledgement audit green today | | 2 | MINOR | Three statements said the document-suite guard "reads `HEAD`'s committed report and fails against it". It does not: `tests/report_markdown_test.rs:85-88` reads the **working tree's** copy (`fs::read_to_string` of `phases/phase-084/<name>`), so the guard can never fail because of `HEAD`'s copy — it fails when the working-tree document is broken, and a committed report that is broken while the working-tree copy is fixed stays green | All three statements now say the guard reads the working tree's copy, and why that is the right copy to guard: the delivery *is* the working tree, and the pipeline copies it into its own repo before the gate runs. `HEAD`'s committed copy was measured against the same checker in this round and passes too — the paste is under "Tests added" below | | 3 | MINOR | Round 7's pipeline half was asserted but unverifiable from this checkout: the commit `52bdf9e` (`rbops/dispatch.sh` +19/−4, `rbops/smoke.sh` +11) and the "232 checks, green" figure live in the pipeline repo, one directory up, outside the checkout, and this reviewer's own permissions denied `external_directory` | The pipeline repo **is** reachable from this checkout. `git -C ../rbops show 52bdf9e` and `bash ../rbops/smoke.sh` were run here, and both outputs are pasted under "The pipeline" below: the commit is in the pipeline repo's history at its `HEAD`, and the suite is green at 232 checks |  ### The MAJOR, in full  The reflog is the whole of it:  ``` $ cat .git/logs/HEAD 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 b21821d... clone: from github.com:authorss81/redblue.git b21821d... 1b58178... commit: resume phase-084: adopt preserved work 1b58178... 1b58178... reset: moving to HEAD 1b581